1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:06,800
Four hundred years ago, one state stretched from the Baltic almost to the Black Sea.

2
00:00:06,800 --> 00:00:14,200
By 1795 it had vanished from the map entirely. How does a giant simply disappear?

3
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:29,729
In 1384, a ten-year-old girl named Jadwiga was crowned “King” of Poland.

4
00:00:29,729 --> 00:00:36,841
To the east lay Lithuania – vast, powerful, and the last pagan state in Europe.

5
00:00:36,841 --> 00:00:44,835
The Union of Krewo arranged a marriage. Jogaila was baptised, married Jadwiga and became King Władysław II Jagiełło.

6
00:00:44,835 --> 00:00:52,241
Together they crushed their common enemy, the Teutonic Knights, at the Battle of Grunwald in 1410.

7
00:00:55,941 --> 00:01:03,347
1569. The last Jagiellonian king, Sigismund II Augustus, had no son. The union risked falling apart…

8
00:01:03,347 --> 00:01:11,747
…so the Union of Lublin merged Poland and Lithuania into one Commonwealth: one parliament, one elected king.

9
00:01:11,747 --> 00:01:19,153
Its lands now belong to Poland, Lithuania, Latvia, Belarus, Ukraine and parts of Russia and Estonia.

10
00:01:19,153 --> 00:01:27,853
Catholics, Orthodox, Protestants, Jews and Muslim Tatars lived side by side. In 1573 the Warsaw Confederation guaranteed religious peace.

11
00:01:31,553 --> 00:01:41,312
With no dynasty left, kings were now elected – by any nobleman who turned up. Tens of thousands voted in a field near Warsaw.

12
00:01:41,312 --> 00:01:50,612
Every new king had to swear to the Henrician Articles, handing much of his power to the nobles.

13
00:01:50,612 --> 00:01:58,606
The nobility – the szlachta – ran the country. Most peasants were serfs, bound to their lords’ land.

14
00:01:58,606 --> 00:02:07,506
Laws needed unanimous consent. From 1652, a single deputy could cancel an entire session of parliament: the liberum veto.

15
00:02:07,506 --> 00:02:15,794
Over the next century, dozens of parliaments ended without passing a single law. Government slowly ground to a halt.

16
00:02:19,494 --> 00:02:28,959
Yet on the battlefield it was feared. At Kircholm in 1605, its winged hussars beat a Swedish army about three times their size.

17
00:02:28,959 --> 00:02:35,188
In 1610 its army even marched into Moscow and occupied the Kremlin.

18
00:02:35,188 --> 00:02:42,212
By 1619 the Commonwealth covered almost one million km² – its greatest extent.

19
00:02:45,912 --> 00:02:53,818
1648. The Cossacks of Ukraine rose up under Bohdan Khmelnytsky against Polish lords and Catholic rule.

20
00:02:53,818 --> 00:03:01,224
In 1654 the Cossacks swore loyalty to the Russian Tsar at Pereiaslav. Russia invaded within months.

21
00:03:01,224 --> 00:03:08,041
Russian armies poured across the border, taking Smolensk, Vilnius and much of the east.

22
00:03:08,041 --> 00:03:15,453
Then in 1655 Sweden invaded too. Warsaw and Kraków fell. Poles call it “the Deluge”.

23
00:03:15,453 --> 00:03:22,271
War, famine and plague killed between a quarter and a third of the population.

24
00:03:22,271 --> 00:03:30,176
The Truce of Andrusovo (1667) handed Smolensk, Kyiv and eastern Ukraine to Russia – for good.

25
00:03:33,876 --> 00:03:39,518
1683. A huge Ottoman army besieged Vienna, the Habsburg capital.

26
00:03:39,518 --> 00:03:45,018
Poland’s King Jan III Sobieski rode to the rescue.

27
00:03:45,018 --> 00:03:54,118
His charge of some 18,000 horsemen – among the largest in history – broke the siege. It was the Commonwealth’s last great triumph.

28
00:03:57,818 --> 00:04:06,400
1697. The Elector of Saxony bought enough votes to be elected King Augustus II. Foreign money now chose Poland’s kings.

29
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:14,988
In the Great Northern War (1700–1721), Swedish and Russian armies marched across Poland as if it were their own.

30
00:04:14,988 --> 00:04:22,100
Peter the Great’s victory at Poltava in 1709 made Russia the master of Eastern Europe.

31
00:04:22,100 --> 00:04:31,000
In 1717 the “Silent Sejm”, surrounded by Russian troops, capped the army at 24,000 men. Russia now “guaranteed” Poland’s freedoms.

32
00:04:30,1000 --> 00:04:38,700
Its neighbours kept armies many times larger.

33
00:04:42,400 --> 00:04:49,806
1764. Russia’s Catherine the Great put her former lover, Stanisław August Poniatowski, on the Polish throne.

34
00:04:49,806 --> 00:04:59,406
1772: the First Partition. Russia, Prussia and Austria seized nearly 30% of its land and over a third of its people.

35
00:04:59,406 --> 00:05:05,047
Europe’s cartoonists drew the monarchs carving up “the Polish cake”.

36
00:05:05,047 --> 00:05:14,447
Shocked into action, Poland passed Europe’s first modern written constitution on 3 May 1791.

37
00:05:14,447 --> 00:05:21,559
Catherine would not allow it. In 1792 Russian troops invaded, and the constitution was crushed.

38
00:05:21,559 --> 00:05:29,959
1793: the Second Partition. Russia and Prussia took another huge share. What was left could barely survive.

39
00:05:29,959 --> 00:05:39,059
1794. Tadeusz Kościuszko, a veteran of the American Revolution, led a national uprising. Peasants armed with scythes won at Racławice.

40
00:05:39,059 --> 00:05:46,171
But in October he was wounded and captured at Maciejowice, and Russian troops stormed Warsaw.

41
00:05:46,171 --> 00:05:54,771
1795: the Third Partition. The Commonwealth was wiped off the map.

42
00:05:54,771 --> 00:06:01,882
For the next 123 years, there would be no Poland on the map of Europe.

43
00:06:29,082 --> 00:06:36,682
Poland finally returned to the map in 1918 – but the old Commonwealth was gone forever.

